WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.340
- What is that?

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:02.570
- That's the baby's buttocks.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.463
She's breech.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.942
- Oh, thank God, I
thought she had two heads.

00:00:05.942 --> 00:00:07.400
- Oh God.
(audience laughs)

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.350
- Let's have a little
fun today and check out

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.960
one of my favorite shows
of all time, Friends.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.680
There's some medical scenes on
there that are questionable,

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.180
and I wanna review them with you.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.111
Let's get started.

00:00:17.111 --> 00:00:17.944
Peewoop!

00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:18.880
- Am I interrupting?

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.580
- Oh no, Dr. Long, please come in.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.910
This is Ross.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:22.850
He is the father.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.490
- But not the husband because evidently,

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.600
she can do this alone.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.860
- I really felt like
I was Ross growing up.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.880
I was the geek, I was the nerd,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.250
and I've always loved Rachel.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:35.220
- There's your uterus, and right here

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.023
is your baby.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.660
- Oh my God.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.890
- Always a really powerful moment

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.660
when parents get to see
the heartbeat of the baby.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.480
I would usually show the
chambers and the heart beating.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.720
Family gets really excited.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.980
It's a special place to be
when you're delivering a baby.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.270
As a family medicine
doctor, I no longer do it,

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.670
even though I'm licensed and I know how.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.698
I've delivered 38 babies in residency.

00:00:58.698 --> 00:01:01.620
(Rachel whimpers)

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.750
- Pretty amazing, huh?

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.700
- I've seen Friends so many times.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.600
I know what she's crying about.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.707
- I don't see it.

00:01:07.707 --> 00:01:09.590
(audience laughs)

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.650
- It's so funny, nowadays,
it would be less of an issue

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.890
because we have like these 3-D ultrasounds

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.560
that can actually show
the face of the baby.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.060
We can print out the pictures

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.680
and then the parents go show
them around to their families.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.513
It's really cute.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.110
- Hi, hi, this is Rachel Green.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:22.943
I'm Ross Geller.

00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:24.140
We, we called from the car.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.229
- Right, we have a semi-private
labor room waiting for you.

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:28.170
So we're just gonna...
- Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.260
whoa, whoa, I'm sorry, semi-private?

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.070
We, we, (laughs) we
asked for a private room.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.670
- Yes, I see that here.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.210
Unfortunately, we can't
guarantee a private room,

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.320
and currently they're all unavailable.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.380
- Hospitals, especially in big
cities, like New York City,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.120
they run out of rooms quickly.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.340
So sometimes, especially
when you first arrive,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.500
you can get only a semi-private room,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.240
and then whoever has the most seniority,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.970
who's been at the hospital the longest,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.510
they may shift you around and
move you to a private room.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:57.343
- Check these out.

00:01:57.343 --> 00:01:58.483
- Uh-huh.

00:01:58.483 --> 00:02:01.316
(audience laughs)

00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:04.610
- It's a funny position for Ross.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.590
Back in the day, these
used to be called stirrups.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.520
We tend not to use that word anymore

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.900
'cause weird connotations.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.910
We now call them leg rests
or ankle rests or foot rests.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.060
- Who's that?

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.323
- New people.

00:02:16.323 --> 00:02:17.600
(audience chuckles)

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.160
- When it's your first pregnancy,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.790
statistically, it takes longer.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.030
You have a longer labor.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.400
If someone's coming in for
their third or fourth baby,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.840
usually the process a lot quicker.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.380
So you're gonna have multiple roommates

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.230
if it's your first.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.940
- You listen to me, since
I have been waiting,

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.170
four women, that's four,

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.890
one higher than the number of
centimeters that I am dilated,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.710
have come and gone with their babies.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.590
I'm next.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:39.620
It's my turn.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.610
It's only fair.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.420
- When she says she's
three centimeters dilated,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.740
what she's talking about is
the opening of the cervix.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.940
That's the opening of the uterus.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.950
The way we monitor the
progression of labor

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.020
is through doing an estimate measurement,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.690
usually through a digital
exam, using our fingers,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.500
to see what that dilation is.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.250
And three centimeters dilated

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.373
is really early in the labor process.

00:03:03.373 --> 00:03:04.306
(Rachel groans)

00:03:04.306 --> 00:03:05.873
- Push.
(Rachel pants)

00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:07.560
Gonna push for five more seconds.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.300
- My favorite part of this is the resident

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.720
or medical student, just standing there.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.450
That is so accurate. (laughs)

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.417
They just stand there aimlessly,
like, "what should I do?

00:03:14.417 --> 00:03:15.250
"I'm learning."

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.030
That was me back in the day.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.900
That was all of us.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.040
The one thing is, I don't
know why the doctor's

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:19.960
not wearing a mask.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:20.920
We wear masks.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.840
- Wait, I see something.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.651
- What'd you do, you do?

00:03:24.651 --> 00:03:25.746
Oh my God!

00:03:25.746 --> 00:03:26.734
- Don't say oh my God.

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:27.786
Oh my God, what?

00:03:27.786 --> 00:03:29.150
- What is that?

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.150
- It's the baby's buttocks.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.053
She's breech.

00:03:31.971 --> 00:03:33.476
- Oh thank God, I thought
she had two heads.

00:03:33.476 --> 00:03:35.390
- Oh God.
(audience laughs)

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.870
- Breech means the baby is
coming out not head first,

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.650
coming out butt first,
which is problematic.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.990
You want the baby to come down

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.560
and descend the canal head first.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.680
A breech has a high rate of complications.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.000
It's gonna be a tricky delivery,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.510
especially if it's this far along.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:54.010
But I love that Ross took
a look and did not faint

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.240
because a lot of fathers,
when they take a look,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.710
they're not ready to see
everything that's going on.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:02.620
They get a vasovagal reaction
where blood pressure drops,

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:03.770
not enough circulation,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.460
not enough blood flow getting
to the head and they go wham.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.980
I always have my nurses
keeping an eye on the dads

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.670
while the mom's delivering.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:11.780
- You're gonna have to push even harder.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:12.613
Nothing's happening.

00:04:12.613 --> 00:04:13.864
- I'm sorry, I can't.

00:04:13.864 --> 00:04:14.697
I can't.
- Yes you can.

00:04:14.697 --> 00:04:15.900
Hey, hey, come on, you can.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:16.851
I know you can do this.

00:04:16.851 --> 00:04:18.344
Let's go.
- I can't, no, I...

00:04:18.344 --> 00:04:19.793
Please, you do it for me.

00:04:19.793 --> 00:04:20.960
- No.
(audience laughs)

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.170
- What they're missing here is

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.230
the mom shouldn't just
be pushing aimlessly.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.880
She should be doing it
along with the contraction.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.320
So it should be time.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.270
We get to see the baby
monitor on the side.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.520
When we see the contraction
starting, mom should feel it,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.950
and at that point, we should
have the mom bear down,

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:39.160
push, count, and maybe
give a second effort there.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.018
But they're just, looks like
they're pushing aimlessly.

00:04:42.018 --> 00:04:42.885
(Rachel laughs)

00:04:42.885 --> 00:04:45.265
- That is not a newborn baby. (laughs)

00:04:45.265 --> 00:04:47.250
- Oh, she, she's perfect.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:48.210
- Oh, wow.

00:04:49.507 --> 00:04:51.323
Oh, she's so tiny.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:57.770
Where'd she go?

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.520
- So we always try and get
the baby on the mom's chest

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.800
as fast as possible.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.370
We feel like the best
sort of effects happen

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.500
when the baby gets right
on the mom's chest.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.980
Mom calms down, it helps
lower her blood pressure,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.070
slow the heart rate down, baby feels mom.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.340
It's like nature at its finest.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.500
And while mom is holding the baby,

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.280
then we let the father step
in and cut the umbilical cord.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.870
Dads panic when they
cut the umbilical cord,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:22.900
'cause it's kind of rubbery

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.200
and it takes a little bit of force

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.570
and they don't wanna hurt anything,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:27.500
so they're really worried about doing it.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.250
It's really cute to
watch, and I love that.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.470
That's like one of my favorite parts,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.276
getting the dad involved to do something.

00:05:32.276 --> 00:05:34.057
- Hi, you're going into what grade?

00:05:34.057 --> 00:05:35.790
(audience laughs)

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.020
- I'm actually a first year
resident, but I get that a lot.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.010
See, I graduated early.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.050
- Uh-huh, uh, me too.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.960
- That's me every time in the hospital.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:43.930
What are you, 10?

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:45.130
Are you in kindergarten?

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.480
What grade are you in?

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.590
Are you like Doogie Howser?

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.280
Then occasionally someone'd
be like, wow, you're young.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:53.350
That means you must be smart,
and I was like, thank you.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.923
- Mr. Tribbiani, I'm afraid
you've got kidney stones.

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.530
- Oh, well, what else could it be?

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:01.640
- It's kidney stones.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:02.860
- To diagnose kidney stones,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.620
sometimes we do an x-ray like that.

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:07.920
Better tests, which we
tend to do more often,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.960
would be a CT scan with
very specific parameters.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.160
We could also get an ultrasound.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.370
All those are options.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:14.905
- Better, sweetie?

00:06:14.905 --> 00:06:16.670
- Maybe a little.

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:17.527
Wish you hadn't seen me throw up.

00:06:17.527 --> 00:06:19.370
- Sometimes the pain can get so bad

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:20.530
that it can lead you to vomit.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.930
I'm gonna give you a
little revelation here.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.560
I've passed kidney stones before.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.950
When I was really young,
didn't drink a lot of water.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.180
I drank a lot of soda and I
passed one of these stones

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.080
and they were tiny, tiny,
tiny, but boy did it hurt.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.010
There was blood.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.860
Oh my God, if you ever have
to pass one, I'm sorry,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.410
'cause it's not a fun experience.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.000
So I sympathize with my patients

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.620
and I empathize with my man, Joey, here.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.730
- Now ordinarily, Mr. Tribbiani,

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:46.980
we try to break the stones
up with shock waves,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.170
but they're too close to the bladder now,

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.860
which means we can either
wait for you to pass them

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.700
or else go up the urethra.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.617
- Whoa, whoa.
(audience laughs)

00:06:56.617 --> 00:06:58.070
No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.038
Nothing is going up, okay?

00:07:00.038 --> 00:07:02.680
Up, up is not an option.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.679
What's a urethra?

00:07:03.679 --> 00:07:05.000
(laughs)

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.010
- Actually, up is sometimes an option

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.650
and sometimes it's something we have to do

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:09.840
to inspect the bladder.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.470
Another potential option for patients

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:12.690
who are looking to pass stones

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.590
is giving them a medication
to help in that process

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.310
to relax the smooth muscles of that area.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.610
- Oh my God.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.276
You did it, man.

00:07:21.276 --> 00:07:22.760
(audience laughs)

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.249
- Would you like to see them?

00:07:24.249 --> 00:07:27.082
(audience laughs)

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.480
- They're so small.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:30.313
- They're not that small.

00:07:30.313 --> 00:07:31.740
Those are fairly big size.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.610
- I was in the shower and
as I was cleansing myself,

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.360
I, I, I, well, I felt something.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.557
- Was it like a sneeze only better?

00:07:40.557 --> 00:07:42.300
(audience laughs)

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.540
- No, no, I mean, I
mean a thing on my body.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.373
- What was it?

00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:48.500
- Well, I (sighs) I don't know.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.550
It's, it's kind of in a place that's not,

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.780
it's not visually accessible to me,

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:57.300
and I was hoping maybe you
guys could, could help me out.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.311
- Whoa!
- Hey!

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:01.560
- (laughs) That's funny,
'cause random people

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.380
tend to approach me and show
me their lesions quite often.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.900
I cannot imagine how
dermatologists must feel.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.844
- That's not a third nipple.

00:08:09.844 --> 00:08:10.677
- Oh?

00:08:10.677 --> 00:08:11.906
- First of all, it's on your ass.

00:08:11.906 --> 00:08:12.790
(audience laughs)

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.050
- Doctors don't say that.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.030
- Johnson would you come in here a moment?

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.350
- [Johnson] I'm with Hamilton.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:18.240
- He's good with weird things.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.073
Bring him in, too.

00:08:19.073 --> 00:08:20.200
(audience laughs)

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:22.730
- It's funny, because anytime
you have a rare condition

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.670
in a hospital, you're gonna get residents,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.820
specialists, fellows, everyone coming in.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.360
Not only to help the
case, but also to learn

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.860
because we learn from our
patients as much as we help them.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.360
(Ross groans)

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:38.143
- You okay, Ross?

00:08:38.143 --> 00:08:39.648
- I don't know. (groans)

00:08:39.648 --> 00:08:41.790
(audience laughs)

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:43.620
What's in this pie?

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.780
- I don't know, butter and eggs and flour

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.627
and lime and kiwi.

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:50.344
- Kiwi, kiwi?

00:08:50.344 --> 00:08:52.110
You said it was a key lime pie.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.470
- No, I didn't, I said kiwi lime.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.220
That's what makes so special.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.560
- That's what's gonna kill me.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.250
(audience laughs)

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.230
- I'm allergic to kiwi.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.300
- Ross is experiencing
an anaphylactic attack.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:07.250
His upper airway is literally swelling.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.780
It's closing up.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.450
That can be quite dangerous quite quickly.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.920
He also has dilation of
all of his blood vessels,

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.110
which drops your blood
pressure quite rapidly.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.840
Your heart rate usually
springs up to compensate

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.250
for this loss of pressure.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:23.390
But sometimes the blood pressure effects

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:27.170
can lead you to faint, vomit,
feel awful, have a headache,

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:29.420
and you desperately need to get to a place

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.990
that has an epinephrine auto injector.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:32.823
- No, you're not.

00:09:32.823 --> 00:09:34.866
You're allergic to lobster and peanuts.

00:09:34.866 --> 00:09:35.802
(Ross groans)

00:09:35.802 --> 00:09:36.635
(laughs)

00:09:36.635 --> 00:09:38.410
- This reminds me of the scene from Hitch.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:39.243
(Hitch gags)

00:09:39.243 --> 00:09:41.030
- Whoa, whoa, you all right?

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:42.150
- Oh, no, yeah.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:43.316
- Is your tongue swelling up?

00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:44.971
- Either that or my
mouth is getting smaller.

00:09:44.971 --> 00:09:46.831
(audience laughs)
- Both.

00:09:46.831 --> 00:09:47.760
- All right, get your coat.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.610
We're gonna go to the hospital.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.930
- You really should call
911 in these situations

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.420
because the ambulance will arrive,

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:56.140
usually with an epinephrine
auto injector on board,

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.760
and they could administer
it if they need to.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.930
The last thing you want is to get in a car

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:01.110
and then the swelling gets worse

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:02.220
and then your throat closes up in the car

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.310
and you don't know what to do.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:05.080
So call 911 for help.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.360
Even if you actually give yourself

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.810
an epinephrine auto injector,

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:10.380
you should still head to the ER.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.230
20% of the time, you may need
either a second injection

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:16.100
or other interventions because
the first one has failed.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.250
You also need to be monitored

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:18.770
after give yourself epinephrine.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.780
Your heart rate, your blood
pressure, all of that.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:22.430
- There's no way I'm gonna
get a shot, all right?

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:23.960
Maybe they can take the needle, you know,

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.270
and squirt it into my
mouth, like a squirt gun.

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:27.360
- You have to give the shot.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.380
You have to give it quickly,

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.480
way quicker than what
he's experiencing here.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.470
And you have to give it
right in the anterior

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.170
lateral portion of your thigh.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.550
That's when you get the most
absorption as fast as possible.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:37.970
Remember, this is an emergency.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.857
- You add a pinch of saffron,
it makes all the difference.

00:10:40.857 --> 00:10:42.420
(audience cheers)

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:45.130
- This is partially the reason

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.750
I really wanted to be a doctor.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:48.820
This scene, the admiration,

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:52.160
Monica and Rachel are giving my man George

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.193
and I forgot the other, John White?

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:56.925
Will?
Noah Wiley.

00:10:56.925 --> 00:10:58.610
(hands clap)
Getting all this attention.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:01.710
I wanted it, mostly because
no one paid attention to me.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.810
Not 'cause I was huge ego.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:05.260
- You're gonna walk over
to the operating table.

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:06.380
You're gonna stop on that blue mark.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:07.670
You're gonna put the tray down.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:11.302
Don't walk too fast, but don't dawdle.

00:11:11.302 --> 00:11:13.330
(audience laughs)

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:14.163
- Okay, now, what?

00:11:14.163 --> 00:11:15.780
- If they're truly
operating on this patient,

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.920
why are all their masks down,

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.760
and why does Phoebe have no gloves on?

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:21.950
I don't like what's happening here.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:24.840
I know this is a show inside
of a show inside of a show

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.400
and we're in inception land,
but I still get frustrated,

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.150
'cause I want you to
have an accurate picture

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:32.310
of what happens in a
hospital system, you dig?

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:33.440
Don't know what I'm doing,

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.690
but it feels like I'm
squaring up the conversation.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:37.980
- How's it going?

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:39.780
- Going really good, but enough about me?

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:41.610
Come on. (laughs)

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:42.926
Where are you from?
What do you do?

00:11:42.926 --> 00:11:44.990
(audience laughs)

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:45.860
- I'm a doctor.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:46.900
- Right, right.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.440
I'm actually, I meant, you
know, in your spare time,

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.920
do you cook, do you ski,

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.232
or just hang out with
your wife or girlfriend?

00:11:53.232 --> 00:11:54.980
(audience laughs)

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.110
- I don't have a wife or
girlfriend, but I do like to ski.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.100
- Oh, I love to ski!

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.050
How amazing is this?

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:02.690
- This conversation legitimately happens.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:05.760
It happens more actually
in the hospital setting.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:07.490
Patients are usually there overnight

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:08.850
or for several days at a time.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:11.210
You get quite lonely, so they
wanna have a conversation.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.120
But when it starts crossing
into that territory

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.190
where they're trying to
get into my personal life

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.900
and see if I'm single, that's
when it gets a little weird

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.340
and boundaries need to be set.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.310
- So, are you experiencing any discomfort?

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:22.610
- No, I'm very comfortable.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.320
- Oh my God, this reminds
me of my interview

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.500
that I just did on The
Daily Show not too long ago.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.950
- How close is too close?

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.220
- Ideally you'd wanna keep six feet away

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.460
from anyone that you're not familiar with.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.083
- Would this be too close?

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:39.030
- In my medical,
professional medical opinion,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:40.130
I would say that's too close.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:43.340
So six feet and then washing your hands.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:44.329
- Is this too close?

00:12:44.329 --> 00:12:45.162
- That would be too close.

00:12:45.162 --> 00:12:46.470
- Too close.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:47.303
- Awkward.

00:12:47.303 --> 00:12:50.110
Here's two throwback
medical drama reviews.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.530
ER, Doogie Howser.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:53.900
Which one are you gonna watch?

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:54.820
Watch them both.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.580
Mm, medical accuracy's not bad on both.

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.090
Which one are you watching?

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.121
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:13:01.121 --> 00:13:03.788
(upbeat music)

